    thinking: "**Tracing Match Loading**\n\nI\'m currently tracing the match loading process. It seems to kick off when players are ready in the draft phase, transitioning to \"deploy\" in `server.ts`. This triggers the `DeployScreen.tsx` component.\n\n\n**Analyzing Deployment Transitions**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the transition to the active battle phase, triggered by both players\' readiness. The `server.ts` endpoint `/api/rooms/:roomId/deploy/ready` calculates bot positions, builds units, and sets the phase to \'active.\' However, the `DeployScreen.tsx` component is causing a delay; setting ready doesn\'t immediately refresh the state. Instead, it relies on a 1500ms polling interval in `App.tsx`, and makes extra network requests such as to the map cover and individual tile images.\n\n\n**Optimizing Room Persistence**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the `server.ts` file and identified a potential bottleneck. The current implementation of `saveRoom` in `server.ts` is synchronously writing to Firestore after *every* API call, leading to network latency issues. This blocking behavior is significant. I\'m considering an alternative: saving to in-memory `rooms[roomId]` immediately and writing to Firestore asynchronously to avoid this bottleneck. The `loadRoom` function already caches rooms in memory, so that helps too.\n\n\n**Refining Match Transition**\n\nI\'m now focusing on optimizing the match transition. I realized that writing to Firestore synchronously after every API call causes significant delays. I\'m prioritizing updating the in-memory room immediately and asynchronously persisting it to Firestore. Moreover, I\'m examining ways to trigger room state updates instantly in `DeployScreen.tsx` and `CreateMatchMenu.tsx`, instead of relying on a 1.5s polling tick. I\'m also looking at preloading map tiles and cover data. Finally, I\'m analyzing a user reported map corruption bug.\n\n**Investigating Map Corruption**\n\nI\'m investigating the map corruption issue in `DeployScreen.tsx`. It appears the `getMapImage(mapId)` function in `useFirebaseImages.ts` is likely the culprit. The code currently displays a potentially undefined image which the user sees. I will examine why this may happen, and if this causes the wrong map image to be rendered, by analyzing where `mapId` is set, and why this happens!\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 5
      nanos: 95820658
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
